  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The route starts in the Pansard valley, at the foot of the Notre-Dame-des-Maures track, and leads to the Gaoutabry dolmen, the largest dolmen in the Var: a Neolithic collective tomb listed on the Historic Monuments inventory, oriented towards the sunset at the winter solstice, which held the cremated remains of at least 34 individuals along with offerings. The dolmen is surrounded by an enclosure that you can walk through to see the remains up close. The circuit crosses vineyards and offers views of the sea, La Londe-les-Maures, the Salins d'Hyères and the Îles d'Or.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The tourist office publishes no dog rule for this route; keep your dog on a leash. The key point: from June to September, before going anywhere in the Var massifs, check the opening conditions on the fire risk map (updated at 5pm each day for the next day) or call the tourist office (04 94 01 53 10). The tourist office's mini-guide reminds visitors that a prefectural order regulates access to the massifs from 15 June to 1 October.</p>

      <h2>On the ground</h2>
      <p>The 7.2 km circuit (2 h 35, 179 m of elevation gain, easy waymarking) first follows the Notre-Dame-des-Maures DFCI track, which rises slowly through the vineyards, then climbs to the top of the hill where the cleared area of the prehistoric site is found. These are wide forest tracks, with little shade on the hill: bring water for your dog, as the tourist office mentions no water point. Driving and parking of any motor vehicle on DFCI tracks is prohibited all year. In an emergency, the office reminds visitors to call 112; mobile coverage may be lacking on some paths in the massif according to the mini-guide. The route is accessible all year, with free access, and graded green (easy), notably for families with children. The route is also offered to mountain bikers: watch out for cyclists on the tracks.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: always check access to the massif in summer before hitting the road.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>La Londe-les-Maures and its vineyards are close by, a good excuse to extend the outing.</p>
